## [ENT-017] | 2026-11-16 18:18 UTC | Unity | order | ref: game/routes/master-index.md | see: ENT-016
Changed: on Suggi's words of 2026-11-16, between 17:00 and 18:20 UTC. (1) He reworked the standing orders: four, in his words; what the earlier S2 to S6 named lives on in the plan, P1 to P5. (2) The Fleet Admiral organizes the fleet and instructs the Admirals toward its goals; the treasury, shared storage and every ship in the faction are her medium-term goals (P5). (3) Every member is measured by the orders: the Measure line of its block; its mission stands in its COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster. (4) Testing is always allowed: the hourly job puts open paying routes and up to two one-ship tests per role into the plans by itself. (5) New since ENT-016: a craft stop and the route kind guard (both not flown yet), the forecast (hive, verb=forecast), a gate against a pilot of ours by name, FLEET.md as a roster, and game/routes/ with one index per group and one plan per role under master-index.md. (6) ENT-016 said its turn was started by hand: it was not, Suggi held it. This entry's turn is the first with the plan files; it is started by hand on his word, and the turn jobs stay paused.
In force from 2026-11-16 18:18 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
His direction for now (Suggi, 2026-11-16): "The goal is become economically positive earners. We must fill all the supply routes, craft, build bases, earn money, level up. The strategical map planning is for me where we expand to and such. You guys optimize the routes and money and evolving the fleet and empire."
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders: the fleet's goals in five steps; Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it together; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The ships should never be idle ... as long as all ships are always leveling and doing stuff"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels and gathers. Rule F8.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 146 of 146 ships at work.
P2. An economy that earns, on routes that are never crowded (S2, S4; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, what we craft, every link of the supply chain filled. A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: steady income per hour above zero in every division, and rising.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). A ship that earns nothing still levels; the next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5).
  Goal: every division names in its report what its pilots' levels gained and its next hull step.
P4. Gather and learn for the stations (S4; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "prepare all the material for 1-2 stations and learn how to build it. I chose location in the future"). One station takes a fee of 5,000,000 cr, one station core and the foundry that makes it (38,000,000 cr and about 30 raw inputs; the tool base_plan with kind=station prints each input, what we hold and what is missing). Standing work gathers the inputs into storage; Logistics keeps the list and tests the crafted steps by hand; Intel finds the sources the list lacks. Two stations take the list twice.
  Goal: per input the units held against the units needed, rising every turn; one recipe step tested per turn.
P5. One fleet (S3, S4): the Fleet Admiral's medium-term goals, each paid step on Suggi's word. The treasury stands today: any member pays in, the leader pays out. Shared storage is a Faction Lockbox at one of our stations (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards). Every ship in the faction takes recruitment offices there, three steps to 200 members (about 2,375,000 cr, 1,275 steel plate, 625 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer; the rent is not read yet). We make the plates and boards ourselves: the first use of our supply chain. The purse on the flagships (rule F20) and more ships (rule F5) stand as they did.
  Goal: 275 steel plate and 75 circuit boards made and stored for the lockbox and the hiring board; the rent read from the game; Suggi's word before each payment.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The turn started by hand on Suggi's word of 2026-11-16 (given between 17:00 and 18:20 UTC): each Admiral writes its division's plan file. Every ship of yours gets its own routes in their order: the paying routes to as many ships as each carries and no more (the column `carries`), the best ships on the routes that need them, every other ship standing work spread over places that hold. Choose the best routes for the plan's steps and the standing orders. No ship holds routes of its own yet; the standing routes and the tests in your lists were put there by the Fleet Admiral and the hourly job: keep, cut or replace them, and say why. Read the forecast of your division before and after you plan (the tool hive with verb=forecast and your name).
  Done when: plan.py check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has routes of its own in the plan file; the Standing Plan in your guidebook names each route with its ships and what it carries; FLEET.md carries your roster (fleetfile.py --commit); the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), every route your ships were put on with the number of ships on each, and the forecast's SUM line of your division before and after.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": read the briefing (preflight prints it; the tool hive with verb=briefing); the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; standing work for every other ship, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md (the roster). What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12; above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12) and the frontier rule (F13). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about an order is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: organize the fleet (Suggi, 2026-11-16): turn the standing orders into the fleet's goals (the plan) and into each Admiral's orders, and instruct the Admirals toward them: an economy that earns, the material for bases, for expanding and for upgrades, levels and hulls. Smooth the Admirals' way and build the plumbing and the systems they work through (Suggi, 2026-11-15): answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; bring to Suggi what is his to decide. Medium term, P5: the treasury, shared storage, every ship in the faction. The command group does standing work and levels.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's steady income per hour; ships at work of 146; the steps of P5 done of the steps named.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the stations take, iron first (431,678 units short for one station at 14:49 UTC), on fields that hold, each with a floor. Say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back.
Measure: income per hour; miners at work, 40 of 40; iron ore held against the 491,400 one station takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and buy the stations' inputs where they are cheap: our books of 14:49 UTC showed iron ore on offer at 1 cr at Central Nexus (35,923 units) and carbon ore at 4 cr at Nova Terra Central (44,531 units). Cost each against mining it, test with one ship and one unit, then buy inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade does standing work. Fly the control-node craft circuit once with one trader: the craft stop stands and is not flown yet (requests ENT-161).
Measure: income per hour from trade; station inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 20 of 20; the craft circuit flown once and its RESULT line read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the stations' list in your guidebook: per input what we hold, where it lies and what is missing (base_plan with kind=station against the storage book). Learn the build: make the first crafted steps by hand as tests, one hauler and one batch each, steel plate from iron ore first (49,140 are needed), and say what each costs in credits, minutes and skill. A hauler without that work gathers. The first plates and boards are the faction's (P5): 275 steel plate and 75 circuit boards for the lockbox and the hiring board, made at one of our stations and stored there; say where they lie.
Measure: inputs of the list with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 20 of 20; steel plate and circuit boards held against 275 and 75.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone does standing work. The stations take creature goods too (creature carapace 35,640, verdigris curd 34,832): where no bid stands for a kill's goods, they go into storage. Fly the guard route once with one drone beside a ship of ours beyond the border: it stands and is not flown yet (requests ENT-165).
Measure: income per hour; drones at work, 30 of 30; ships lost, which stays 0; the guard route flown once and its RESULT line read.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: keep the census fresh: it measures what a field and a herd regain, so every place our routes use is read twice in a turn. Find the sources the stations' list lacks: thirteen inputs have no field and no offer in our readings (salvage metal, anchor plate, alloy scale, foil scale, silica lens among them; base_plan lists them). The three station sites you named stay on record; Suggi chooses later. A scout between two reads hunts or does standing work. The faction's offices (P5) take silicon ore (454 units; we hold 34) and caustic sac (450; we hold none): name a source for each.
Measure: places our routes use that were read twice in the turn; inputs of the list with a known source; scouts at work, 20 of 20.

## [ENT-018] | 2026-11-16 20:52 UTC | Unity | order | ref: game/routes/master-index.md | see: ENT-017
Changed: on Suggi's words of 2026-11-16, between 18:35 and 20:30 UTC. (1) Nothing is bad for good (rule F22, new): the game changes by the hour, so no file marks a route as not paying. What a route earns is read every turn from the fleet's database with its age (the tool sql, view route_earnings: the last 4, 12 and 24 hours and the hours since it last flew; the tool routes). A route's measured figure rests on its runs of the last 12 hours; older, it is taken out and the route is a test again. The plan pages carry no credits. The guidebooks lost the section What Did Not Pay; in its place stands Route Templates. (2) Levels by role (rule F23, new): a ship levels the skills of its own role, gathering ore is the fallback, and a scout scans all the time. (3) Templates: a division's named lists of routes; a ship's row in the plan file names one as @name, so one word switches a ship to other work (the tool plan with action=templates, template, use). (4) The reserve and the surplus (rule F24, new): game/reserve.tsv lists what our bases and upgrades take; what we hold above it is surplus and is sold (the tool stock with view=reserve and view=surplus). Trade sells the surplus and writes the sell routes, Logistics makes what we build with, every ship sells its own stores. (5) The fleet is 246 ships: 100 were born on his word (10 sentinels, 20 miners, 20 traders, 20 haulers, 20 drones, 10 scouts) and stand in the plan files. (6) New since ENT-017: the fleet's database holds the current state beside the history (ships, holdings, order books, the catalog, the map, stations, our empire, routes, plans; the tool sql); the inventory (the tool stock with view=inventory or stations); governance/empire.md; the shared skill collective-play (how a member decides alone and when it asks); 25 official facts in game/rules/ at game version 0.719.3. (7) Fixed: a plan that passes its check is flown at once and a guidebook in work holds no plan change (requests ENT-171); a refused call no longer ends a turn; the storage book keeps a pilot that is not connected for a moment (it had fallen from 336,568 to 131,954 units between 20:20 and 20:38 UTC; mended at 20:44 UTC, and a full pass over the fleet's stores takes about 35 minutes). (8) The turn jobs of the five Admirals run on their schedule again, on Suggi's word.
In force from 2026-11-16 20:52 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
His direction for now (Suggi, 2026-11-16): "The goal is become economically positive earners. We must fill all the supply routes, craft, build bases, earn money, level up. The strategical map planning is for me where we expand to and such. You guys optimize the routes and money and evolving the fleet and empire." And on the same day: "They should always explore all, and then put their ships on the stuff they identified each turn. That is why 4h turns, because the game is dynamic."
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders: the fleet's goals in five steps; Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it together; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The goal is farming and optimizing the level of each ship based on what the admirals roles are"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels the skills of its own role; gathering ore is the fallback. Rules F8 and F23.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work; no ship gathers ore while work of its role stands open.
P2. An economy that earns, on routes that are never crowded, judged on what they earn now (S2, S4; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
