P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The goal is farming and optimizing the level of each ship based on what the admirals roles are"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels the skills of its own role; gathering ore is the fallback. Rules F8 and F23.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work; no ship gathers ore while work of its role stands open.
P2. An economy that earns: saturate the supply chains where we are present, and go further for better work (S1, S2, S4, G3; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"; "explore and saturate the profitable economic supply chains in our system where we are present"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space (rule F3), what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. Two divisions do not fill one bid, field or herd: a claim in admirals.log says who takes it (rule F25). A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: steady income per hour above zero in every division, and rising; the bids, fields and herds we fill of those that pay, per system we work in.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). The next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5). Each kind of work is a template with the skills it trains.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its role gained in the turn and its next hull step.
P4. Stations in the region between (G3, S4). Suggi named the sites: Yildun (11 jumps from home) and Aldebaran (9 jumps), then the region of Hawthorne and Alchiba (13 and 14 jumps). All four lie in lawless space between ours and the Outer Rim, and we have read none of them yet (game/map/sites.tsv). The way there: (a) Intel reads every system on the roads from our border to the sites (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): places, fields, herds, stations, order books, who is there. (b) The divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay, from our border outward, so that the road to a station earns. (c) The reserve (game/reserve.tsv) holds what one station takes: the tool base_plan with kind=station prints the fee, the part, the facility that makes it and every input down to the ore; standing work gathers what is short, Logistics makes the crafted steps, and nothing of the reserve is sold (rule F24). (d) Suggi says where and when the first station is founded.
  Goal: systems read of the systems on the roads to Yildun and to Aldebaran; routes that pay beyond our border, in number; per input of one station the units held against the units needed.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2, S3): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own goal. Shared storage first: a Faction Lockbox (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards), which every other faction facility needs. Then five levels of recruiting office, each an upgrade of the one before (governance/empire.md, from the game's catalog): 1 Hiring Board, 50 members (75,000 cr, 75 steel plate, 25 circuit boards); 2 Recruitment Desk, 100 (300,000 cr, 200 plate, 100 boards); 3 Recruitment Center, 200 (2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer); 4 Guild Hall Recruiting, 400 (8,000,000 cr, 3,000 plate, 1,500 boards, 800 titanium alloy); 5 Grand Recruitment Bureau, 1,000 (20,000,000 cr, 8,000 plate, 5,000 boards, 2,500 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate). All of it: 30,575,000 cr, 12,475 steel plate, 7,175 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer, 3,300 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate, and each office's rent. The faction's limit reads 50 today with 16 members; we hold 275 plate and 1 board, and silicon ore is what the boards wait for. The treasury stands: every member ship pays its share in, later. Each payment is made on Suggi's word (rule F11).
  Goal: circuit boards held against 75 (the lockbox and level 1); then per level the materials held against the materials needed; Suggi's word before each payment.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The scheduled turns from 2026-11-16 23:00 UTC: each Admiral sets its division up for work by templates. (a) Read what every route earns now from the database and look at all that is open to your ships, whatever it earned before. (b) Make your division's templates: one for each kind of work, with its routes in the order a ship tries them and the skills it trains (the tool plan with action=template), and put every ship on templates (action=use); a ship may carry routes of its own beside one. The ships born today fly the routes most ships of their role had: give each its work. (c) Rewrite your guidebook's section 1 as route guides (how each kind of route is flown, its fit and skills; a figure there is a dated reading) and section 4 as a levelling guide per kind of ship; take every verdict that a route does not pay out of the book. (d) Level by role: move ships that gather ore to work of your role wherever such work is open.
  Done when: the plan check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has a template or routes of its own; action=templates shows your templates, each with what it trains; your guidebook holds no list of routes that did not pay; FLEET.md carries your roster; the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), the forecast's SUM line before and after, and what the skills of your role gained.
T2. New this turn, on Suggi's words. (a) Claim what you fill: read the claims in force at the start (python3 -B scripts/comms.py admirals) and leave a claimed route to its claimer, or ask; before your session entry write one claim in communications/admirals.log for the routes and places your plan fills until the next turn (the session-end skill has the command). What you need from another Admiral you ask that Admiral in requests.log. (b) Your report carries the section Feedback after Suggestions: whether what the Fleet Admiral built or changed worked in your hands, with the call made and what it answered. (c) Name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better this turn and one you do not master yet (G4). (d) What you ask for and can build yourself, build: a runner, a route, a skill of your own, a figure (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); a request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she may change. (e) Look beyond our space for work of your role (rule F3): one ship, one test, the result read.
  Done when: T1 is done; your claim stands in admirals.log; your report passes the format gate with its Feedback section.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play says what is yours, what is the Fleet Admiral's and what is Suggi's). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": the briefing; what every route earns now; the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; work of your role for every other ship, as templates, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md. Sell what your own ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes. What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you. Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, and judge your own routes, levels and results yourself before you report them.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F26); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the grand plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through, and learn (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Every turn and at every session-end: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback, and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; improve the fleet's database with the inputs it lacks and the tools and scripts that gather them; refresh the catalog and every other record when the game publishes an update, and mend the scripts that read them; keep the money figures right; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; bring to Suggi what is his to decide. Standing goals: the ladder to 1,000 members (P5) and the stations in the region between (P4). The command group guards and hunts for its role; it mines ice where a bid stands.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's steady income per hour; ships at work of 246; per turn, what the database gained and whether the catalog on file is the game's; materials held against the lockbox and level 1 of the ladder; systems read on the roads to the sites.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore first (the faction's circuit boards wait for them; requests ENT-168), then iron. Iron bought by Trade counts toward the reserve like iron gathered: weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. A field beyond our space that holds what a station pays for or what the reserve is short of is yours to test (rule F3).
Measure: income per hour; miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held by the fleet against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where, so that every division's ships sell their own. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good and read what it costs and when it fills. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered (iron at 1 cr: 24,442 units are in; silicon ore next), inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Trade wherever a book pays: a station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit (rules F3, F12).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; it follows one recipe per input, so read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: the 75 circuit boards for the lockbox and the hiring board first, stored beside the 275 steel plate. Four recipes make boards by hand: two take silicon ore (2 for 3 boards with 12 carbon ore, or 2 for 2 boards with 3 copper ore and 1 energy crystal), one takes gold ore in its place (3 copper ore, 3 gold ore and 1 energy crystal for 2 boards), one takes salvage. Buying an input for them inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Read what one station takes (the tool base_plan with kind=station; every recipe of each input with the tool catalog) and say in your report which steps we can make today, which we cannot, and why.
Measure: circuit boards held against 75; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone hunts for its combat skills where a herd holds, and gathers ore only when no herd and no post is open. What a kill yields and the reserve takes (creature carapace, verdigris curd, crystallized biogas) goes into storage where no bid stands for it. Say what a guard prevents and what it costs: the first guard flight stood beside its ship in 11 of 61 looks. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself.
Measure: income per hour; reserve inputs stored per hour; drones at work, 50 of 50; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time. Give every scout routes of its own, as templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to are read every hour, and a scout that ends one read starts the next, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. Keep about ten scouts exploring the galaxy and about twenty where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first every system on the roads to Yildun and to Aldebaran, then on to Hawthorne and Alchiba (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn in the system's file; say in your report what each site and each system on the way holds. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: caustic sac (429 short for the offices) and silicon ore first. The three station sites you named stay on record beside the four Suggi named. Your turn of 18:23 UTC ended without a report on a fault of the tools, which is fixed: this turn also gives every scout routes of its own.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read on the roads to the sites.

